course: biology
lesson: 73
title: Evolution simply: why living things change over many generations
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Look at a litter of puppies: no two are exactly alike, because every baby gets a fresh shuffle of its parents' genes, the instructions for building a body.
explain: If some of those differences help an animal survive and raise more young, the next generation has a little more of them, and over thousands of generations small nudges add up to big changes; this is natural selection.
explain: Nobody chooses the change and no animal tries to change: dark moths were simply eaten less often on sooty trees, so more dark moths were left to have young.
explain: In games and cartoons a creature «evolves» in a flash of light into a new form; real evolution never happens to one animal in its life, it happens to a whole population over many generations.
fact: Babies get a mix of genes from both parents, and small new changes sometimes appear, so brothers and sisters differ; these differences are the raw material of evolution.
fact: In England in the 1800s soot blackened tree trunks, birds ate more pale peppered moths, and dark moths became common; after clean air laws in the 1950s and 1960s pale moths became common again.
fact: Darwin's finches on the Galapagos Islands have beaks suited to their food, and Peter and Rosemary Grant saw the average beak size change within a few years after a drought in 1977.
fact: All pet dogs come from wolves and are still one species; people choosing which dogs had puppies, called artificial selection, made chihuahuas and Great Danes in a few thousand years.
fact: Whales are mammals whose ancestors walked on land about 50 million years ago; their back legs shrank to small hidden bones, and their closest living land relative is the hippopotamus.
fact: Giraffes with slightly longer necks reached more food and had more young; the old idea that giraffes passed on necks they stretched in life is wrong, and a giraffe neck has only 7 bones, like ours.
fact: Bacteria can divide as often as every 20 minutes, so a few tough survivors of antibiotics can multiply into resistant bacteria within days, which is why medicine must be taken exactly as the doctor says: stopping early can leave the toughest bacteria alive to multiply.
fact: Humans and chimpanzees share a common ancestor from about 6 to 7 million years ago, so they are cousins; humans did not come from today's chimps.
fact: Polar bears evolved from brown bears within the last few hundred thousand years; their fur is made of clear hairs and their skin is black.
fact: Mexican tetra fish trapped in dark caves evolved to lose their eyes and colour, because eyes are useless in darkness and cost energy; they feel movements with sensors along their sides.
fact: Charles Darwin sailed on HMS Beagle from 1831 to 1836 and gathered evidence for over 20 years before he published On the Origin of Species in 1859; Alfred Russel Wallace found the same idea on his own, and both were presented together in 1858.
fact: Broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, kale, Brussels sprouts and kohlrabi are all one species, bred by farmers from wild cabbage that grows on European coastal cliffs.
exercise: Why does no puppy in a litter look exactly like another? | Each puppy gets its own mix of genes from both parents | The puppies choose their own colours and patterns before they are born | The mother paints them while they sleep | Only the first puppy gets the parents' genes
exercise: What is natural selection? | Animals with helpful differences survive and have more young | Animals decide to change their bodies whenever they need to | A scientist chooses which animals may have babies | Every animal changes into a new kind during its own life
exercise: Why did dark peppered moths become common in England in the 1800s? | Birds ate more pale moths on soot-blackened trees | Soot from the factory chimneys painted the pale moths dark | The moths chose dark wings to match the trees | Dark moths flew to England from abroad
exercise: What happened to the moths after clean air laws? | Pale moths became common again | All the moths died out in the clean air | The moths all turned green like the leaves | Dark moths became even more common
exercise: What did Peter and Rosemary Grant see in Galapagos finches after the 1977 drought? | The average beak size changed within a few years | The finches grew bigger wings and flew away | Every finch lost its beak | Nothing changed, as evolution takes millions of years
exercise: How did people make chihuahuas and Great Danes from wolves? | By choosing which dogs had puppies over thousands of years | By feeding some dogs much more and others much less for a few years | By crossing wolves with cats and foxes | By training puppies to grow bigger or smaller
exercise: What do the tiny hip bones inside a whale show? | Its ancestors walked on land with back legs | Whales are slowly getting ready to grow back legs again | Whales are really fish with extra bones | Whales use them to walk on the sea floor
exercise: What is the whale's closest living land relative? | The hippopotamus | The dolphin's cousin the seal | The African elephant | The giant river otter
exercise: Why is the idea that giraffes got long necks by stretching wrong? | Stretching in life does not change genes passed to young | Giraffes never stretch their necks at all, not even for leaves | Necks grow only from eating leaves | Giraffe necks have hundreds of bones
exercise: How many bones are in a giraffe's neck? | 7, the same as a human neck | 70, one for every few centimetres | 1, a single very long bendy bone | 25, more than any other animal
exercise: Why can bacteria evolve resistance in days? | They divide very quickly, sometimes every 20 minutes | They learn all about the medicine by talking to each other | They grow armour when they see a doctor | They only live for one day
exercise: How are humans and chimpanzees related? | They are cousins with an ancestor from about 6 to 7 million years ago | Humans are descended from the chimpanzees that live in Africa today | Chimpanzees are descended from humans | They are not related at all
exercise: What animal did polar bears evolve from? | Brown bears | Seals | Wolves | Pandas
exercise: Why did cave fish lose their eyes? | Eyes are useless in darkness and cost energy to grow | The cave water slowly dissolved their eyes over many years | They kept their eyes shut until they disappeared | Bats ate the eyes of every fish
exercise: On which ship did Darwin sail around the world? | HMS Beagle | HMS Victory | The Mayflower | The Titanic
exercise: Who came up with natural selection on his own at the same time as Darwin? | Alfred Russel Wallace | Edward Jenner | Isaac Newton | Gregor Mendel
exercise: What do broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower and kale have in common? | They were all bred from wild cabbage and are one species | They are four very distant kinds of plant that only look a bit alike | They all came from wild lettuce | They all grow wild exactly as we buy them
test: In a game, a creature glows and «evolves» into a new form in one battle. What is wrong? | Evolution happens to whole populations over many generations | Nothing, real animals can change into a new form when they win a fight | It should take a whole week, not one battle | Only plants evolve, never animals
test: «my uncle says we came from monkeys, so why are there still chimps» What is right? | We did not come from today's chimps; both lines are cousins | Chimps are humans who stopped evolving long ago and stayed in the trees | Chimps will turn into humans soon | Humans came from today's gorillas instead
test: «if I do loads of push-ups my kids'll be born strong, right» What is right? | No, muscles built in life do not change the genes you pass on | Yes, every bit of exercise you do is passed on to your children | Yes, but only push-ups, not running | No, children are always weaker than their parents, whatever they do
test: What would happen to a bright green caterpillar living on brown bark with many birds around? | Birds would spot it easily, so fewer green ones would survive | It would turn itself brown in a few minutes on purpose to hide | Birds never eat caterpillars, so nothing | It would grow a hard shell to protect itself
test: «doc said finish all the antibiotics but I feel fine, sod it» Why finish the course? | Stopping early can leave the toughest bacteria alive to multiply | The last pills taste best | Antibiotics only start working properly on the very last day of the course | It doesn't matter, bacteria cannot change
test: Why would a fast-breeding creature like a bacterium evolve faster than an elephant? | Many more generations pass in the same time, so changes add up | Bacteria are much cleverer than elephants and plan their changes | Elephants do not have genes | Big animals are too heavy to change
test: «polar bears are white bears with white skin, obviously» What is right? | Their fur is made of clear hairs and their skin is black | Yes, white fur on white skin, like snow | Their fur is coated in white snow that never melts | Their skin is pink and their fur is grey
test: A farmer wants a cabbage with huge flower buds. What should she do? | Keep seeds from the plants with the biggest buds, year after year | Shout at the plants every morning until they grow bigger buds out of fear | Plant them upside down | Water them with milk
test: A chihuahua and a Great Dane sniff each other at the park. What is true? | They are the same species, both descended from wolves | They are different species that just look alike | The chihuahua came from a fox | The Great Dane came from a horse
test: «Darwin just made it up one day, didn't he» What is right? | He gathered evidence for over 20 years before publishing in 1859 | Yes, he dreamt the whole idea on the Beagle one stormy night | He copied it from Wallace's book | He made it up in 1950
test: Why would a sea animal like a whale still breathe air with lungs? | Its ancestors were land mammals with lungs | Whales are fish that learned to hold their breath | Whale gills stopped working in the last century | Lungs are better than gills for drinking sea water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the process of natural selection? | Variants conferring an advantage in survival become more frequent over generations | Individual organisms deliberately alter their bodies in response to need | Characteristics acquired by exercise during life are transmitted to offspring | A single organism is transformed into a new species within its own lifetime
exam: Identify the principal reason for which dark peppered moths increased in frequency in nineteenth-century England. | Predation by birds fell more heavily upon pale moths on soot-darkened trunks | Soot deposited upon the moths permanently coloured their wings | The moths elected to darken their wings so as to match the trees upon which they rested | Dark moths migrated in great numbers from continental Europe
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the relationship between humans and chimpanzees. | The two share a common ancestor which lived some six to seven million years ago | Humans are directly descended from the chimpanzees of the present day | Chimpanzees are directly descended from early humans living in Africa some million years ago | No relationship of descent exists between the two
exam: State the name of the publication of Charles Darwin which appeared in the year 1859. | On the Origin of Species | The Descent of the Beagle | The Natural History of Selborne | The Voyage of the Galapagos
